This is the exercism forth exercise. Key points:

- `Forth(input []string) ([]int, error)` — each element of input is a line.
- Words are case-insensitive.
- Numbers: a sequence of one or more ASCII digits, optionally with a leading `-` (negative numbers).
- Words: sequences of characters that are not numbers.
- Built-ins: +, -, *, /, dup, drop, swap, over.
- Definition: `: name definition... ;` — the colon and semicolon are words.
- Local scope: "can use different words with the same name" and "can define a word that uses a word of the same name" — when defining `: foo foo 1 + ;`, the body must reference the *old* foo. That is, the body's words are resolved at definition time (immediate execution) and stored as closures.

Implementation approach: represent each word as a function `func(f *forth) error`, or as a slice of tokens. The simplest is: a map from lowercase name to `func() error` that operates on the stack.

