Note: `f.stack` may be nil when empty — the test expects `[]int{}` for "1 drop". reflect.DeepEqual(nil, []int{}) is false. So return a non-nil empty slice. Initialize with `stack: []int{}` and never return nil.

Tokenization: split on whitespace. Number regex: `^-?\d+$`. But the spec says "a number is a sequence of one or more (ASCII) digits" — negative numbers are handled by a leading `-`. The test cases include negative numbers. So treat `-` followed by digits as a number.

Definition: `: name ... ;` — collect tokens until `;`. Name must not be a number (error "cannot redefine a number"). Body tokens are resolved at definition time into a closure that calls the resolved functions.

Local scope test: `: foo 10 ;` then `: foo foo 1 + ;` — the body `foo` must resolve to the old foo. So during definition, resolve each body token to a function value (or number) at that moment. Store the body as a list of "instructions": either a value to push or a function to call.

